1
00:00:00,260 --> 00:00:04,860
今天和我一起的是 Marvin Schwaibold，他是电商平台 Shopify 的产品设计工作室负责人。
Today, I'm joined by Marvin Schwaibold, who leads the product design studio at Shopify.

2
00:00:05,420 --> 00:00:16,700
他将带我们一窥他的幕后流程，看看他如何用最新的 AI 工具把设计变成现实，然后点评一些观众提交的网站。欢迎收看新一期 Design Review（设计评审节目）。
He'll give us a behind the scenes look at his process, where we'll see how he's using the latest AI tools to bring his designs to life, then review some viewer submitted websites. Welcome to another episode of Design Review.

3
00:00:24,160 --> 00:00:25,440
Marvin，感谢你来。
Marvin, thanks for joining us.

4
00:00:25,640 --> 00:00:26,360
感谢你邀请我。
Thank you for having me.

5
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:33,320
在聊你在 Shopify 的工作之前，我很想多听听你现在的设计流程，以及你如今用 AI 做设计时使用的工具。
Before we get into your work at Shopify, I'd love to hear more about your process and the tools that you're using today to design with AI.

6
00:00:33,560 --> 00:00:36,680
我觉得流程本身其实没怎么变。
I feel like the process itself hasn't really changed.

7
00:00:36,840 --> 00:00:41,040
流程依然是不断迭代、按步骤推进。
It's still extremely iterative and sequenced out.

8
00:00:41,120 --> 00:00:44,720
工作本身就有大量的迭代和变化。
There's a lot of iterations and variations in the work in and of itself.

9
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:58,120
但工具变化很大：过去主要用 Figma（界面设计工具）做视觉布局，现在更多采用 AI 驱动的工作流。我会把 Figma 当作草稿本，梳理想法。
But the tooling has definitely shifted a lot from tools like Figma and working with aesthetic layouts into more of an AI-driven workflow where I'll use Figma as a scratch pad for some of the ideas I have.

10
00:00:58,720 --> 00:01:03,240
但很快，这些想法就会变成我可以实际操作的演示。
But then very quickly that'll transcend into real demos that I can use.

11
00:01:03,560 --> 00:01:09,489
之后我只是偶尔回到 Figma，需要排版或向 AI 解释某些东西的时候才用。
And then I only piggyback into Figma from time to time if I need to lay something out or explain something to AI.

12
00:01:09,769 --> 00:01:25,249
但现在更多会用到 Codex（AI 编程工具）和 Claude Code（AI 编程助手），也经常在终端里操作，能更快做出可以在手机或电脑上使用和测试的东西。
But it's definitely more of a world now that includes tools like Codex and Claude Code and being in terminal a lot, and just getting to an output that I can use and test on my phone or computer a lot faster.

13
00:01:25,289 --> 00:01:28,689
这就形成了一个不断迭代、也很有趣的循环。
And that becomes like a really fun, still very iterative cycle.

14
00:01:29,020 --> 00:01:34,340
但我们确实总体上更多地从画布转向了 AI 工具。
But we've definitely moved away from the canvas more to AI tools in general.

15
00:01:34,580 --> 00:01:40,260
那在这个工作流里，有多少是由 AI 在推动，又有多少是由你这个人来推动？
And how much of that workflow is AI driving a lot of the process, and how much of it is you, the human?

16
00:01:40,660 --> 00:01:45,180
我觉得更多是我这个人在驱动 AI，让它替我完成大量重活。
I think it's a lot of me, the human, driving AI to do a lot of the heavy lifting for me.

17
00:01:45,900 --> 00:01:51,420
但大量质量控制和决策仍然都取决于设计师。
But there's a lot of quality controls, decision making that are all up to the designers.

18
00:01:51,660 --> 00:01:57,520
我们坐在驾驶座上，而且现在比以往任何时候都更处在一个“输出不再是问题”的位置。
We're in the driving seat and we're actually more than ever now in a position where the output isn't the issue anymore.

19
00:01:57,960 --> 00:02:03,840
问题在于我们要对推进什么、使用什么极其挑剔。
It's us being extremely selective about what we go forward with and what we work with.

20
00:02:03,960 --> 00:02:10,760
所以更多是在筛选和打磨 AI 创建的内容，并且这是一个持续不断与 AI 来回互动的过程。
So it's a lot more curating what AI is creating, and that is a continuous back and forth with AI.

21
00:02:10,880 --> 00:02:16,200
我觉得 AI 非常擅长完成很多我们过去需要做的事情。
I feel like AI is extremely capable of getting a lot of things done that we needed to do.

22
00:02:16,480 --> 00:02:28,680
只要你理解任务的复杂性，并能把它拆成一个个子任务，再逐步交给 Agent（智能体），就可以……
Um, as long as you understand the complexity of what it is that you're working on, and you're able to break that down into a multitude of almost like sub tasks that then you can feed to the agent bit by bit. Um, and then it's.

23
00:02:28,680 --> 00:02:29,920
它是一种来回互动。
It's a back and forth.

24
00:02:30,320 --> 00:02:33,680
听起来你描述的很多内容，其实是在说你的角色更像是编辑。
And it sounds like a lot of what you're describing is that your role is actually to be the editor.

25
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:36,040
我们现在比以往任何时候都更像是编辑。
We're more the editors now than ever before.

26
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:38,400
我觉得这很有意思。
Um, which is, I think, fun.

27
00:02:38,440 --> 00:02:38,880
这很棒。
That's great.

28
00:02:38,920 --> 00:02:54,360
我的意思是，这个角色一直都有，但以前要达到能编辑的阶段，需要在 Figma 里做大量手工设计；现在越来越多这类工作可以通过 AI 工具加速和扩展。所以……
I mean, that was always the role, but getting to the point of editing just took a lot of like handcrafted design that we would do in Figma. And just more and more of that can now be sped up and extrapolated with AI tooling. And so you.

29
00:02:54,400 --> 00:03:02,360
人仍然要负责最终输出，并确保它始终达到你的标准，但你现在有了这些工具，能更快地达到那个标准。
The human is still responsible for the ultimate final output and making sure that it meets your bar always, but you have all these tools now to be able to get there way faster.

30
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:03,280
百分之百同意。
100%.

31
00:03:03,280 --> 00:03:08,240
我的意思是，AI 确实抬高了我们的上限，但它不是真正的手艺人。
I mean, AI has definitely raised the ceiling for all of us, but it's not a crafter.

32
00:03:08,360 --> 00:03:15,800
呃，它可以执行我无法处理的复杂性和技术。我不会手写代码。
Uh, it can execute complexity and technology that I couldn't. I couldn't hand write code.

33
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:19,120
它能写代码，但不知道什么好看、什么不好看。
It can write code, but it doesn't know what looks good and what looks bad.

34
00:03:19,480 --> 00:03:22,680
这就得由我来判断，并引导 AI 朝正确的方向走。
And that's for me to decide and to steer AI in the right direction.

35
00:03:23,070 --> 00:03:24,750
我一直很喜欢你的作品。
Been a big fan of your work for a long time.

36
00:03:24,830 --> 00:03:30,030
也许先介绍一下你在 Shopify 的产品设计工作室，以及你们在那里做什么。
So maybe first just start off and tell us about the product design studio at Shopify and what you guys do there.

37
00:03:30,150 --> 00:03:32,270
我和一群非常有才华的设计师一起工作。
I work with a team of very talented designers.

38
00:03:32,830 --> 00:03:34,310
我们过去一年都在那里。
We've been there for the last year.

39
00:03:34,630 --> 00:03:44,670
我们几个人基本上在公司推动一个项目，重新设计和触及我们最重要的界面。
And between us, we basically run an initiative at the company to redesign and touch the most important surfaces we have.

40
00:03:45,040 --> 00:03:54,280
从与 Sidekick（Shopify 的 AI 助手）相关的一切开始——它基本是管理我们整个后台的 AI 基础设施层——再到我们的移动 app，我们称之为 green app。
So from anything related to Sidekick, which is our AI infrastructure layers that basically manage our entire admin, to the mobile app, which we call the green app.

41
00:03:54,840 --> 00:04:06,680
从后台到后台仪表盘，所有这些东西我们都在关注和推进。最近我们一直在使用工程团队为我们构建的大量新 AI 基础设施。
And everything from admin and admin dashboards, we look at and work with. Quite recently, we have been working with a lot of new AI infrastructure that the engineering teams have built for us.

42
00:04:07,130 --> 00:04:10,570
嗯，总之过去一年真的非常令人兴奋。
Um, and yeah, it's just been a really exciting last year.

43
00:04:10,730 --> 00:04:15,450
是啊，这确实很有意思，因为 Shopify 现在已经差不多有二十年历史了吧？
Yeah, it's actually really interesting because Shopify is like two decades old or something at this point, right?

44
00:04:15,730 --> 00:04:16,250
20 年了。
20 years old.

45
00:04:16,450 --> 00:04:16,930
20 年了。
20 years old.

46
00:04:17,170 --> 00:04:21,450
所以我觉得，确实，我们正处在一个新时代，AI 正在改变很多事情。
And so, I think, yeah, we're in a new era now where AI is changing a lot of things.

47
00:04:21,450 --> 00:04:31,160
听起来你们做的很多工作，实际上是要通过设计以及体验本身，让它现代化、进入一个现代时代，对吧？
And it sounds like a lot of the work that you're doing is actually to modernize and bring it into a modern era, which is through design but also the experience itself, right?

48
00:04:31,240 --> 00:04:35,320
那设计团队和你的产品设计工作室实际上具体做些什么？
Like what does the design team and your product design studio actually work on there?

49
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:40,640
你们是不是只是待在 Figma、画布和设计稿里，做完后交给工程师？
Are you just, you know, in Figma and canvases and designs and passing them off to engineers?

50
00:04:40,760 --> 00:04:45,000
或者，至少目前你在那里管理怎样的设计职能？
Or what is the design function like that you manage there, at least at the moment?

51
00:04:45,040 --> 00:04:49,120
我们参与的这个大项目几乎可以分成两个部分。
There's almost like two parts of this big initiative that we're taking part of.

52
00:04:49,120 --> 00:04:53,820
其中一个部分是设计团队，他们重新设计了整个后台体验。
One part of the organization, the design team, has redesigned the entire admin experience.

53
00:04:54,220 --> 00:04:59,660
这次改版从 Figma 开始，先打磨界面，再把它带到 Web 上。
This redesign starts in Figma with polishing interfaces and bringing that into the web.

54
00:04:59,780 --> 00:05:17,730
另一个部分，也就是 Shopify 的 Sidekick 团队，正在通过聊天来自动化和操控大量 Shopify 界面，这样你就可以用 Chat Sidekick 或 AI Infra 来管理商店，并自动化很多……
And the other part of the organization, the Sidekick team of Shopify, is working on automating and manipulating a lot of the Shopify interfaces via chat so that you can use Chat Sidekick or AI Infra to basically manage your store and automate a lot of what it.

55
00:05:17,730 --> 00:05:19,770
作为一个在线商家意味着的事情。
means to be a merchant online.

56
00:05:19,970 --> 00:05:29,090
它们基本上是公司里两个几乎对立、充满矛盾的部门，但它们协同得很好。
And they're basically two almost controversial, paradoxical sectors of the company, but they work really well together.

57
00:05:29,250 --> 00:05:37,299
所以你们一方面在打造非常漂亮的新 UI，另一方面也在用新 UI 来生成 AI infra 和 AI 工作流。
So you're building really beautiful new UI, but you're also using new UI to basically generate AI infra and AI workflows.

58
00:05:37,579 --> 00:05:40,259
嗯，这真的很令人惊叹。
Um, and that's like incredible to see.

59
00:05:40,379 --> 00:05:43,379
很多这些成果实际上也能很好地反哺回移动 app。
And a lot of that actually translates very nicely back into the mobile app.

60
00:05:43,859 --> 00:05:47,179
嗯，而且它也在提升移动端的体验。
Um, and it is up leveling the mobile experience as well.

61
00:05:47,299 --> 00:05:54,419
这种方式更偏对话，更多是和 Sidekick 聊你的业务，而不是通过界面去操作你的业务。
It's a lot more conversational and a lot more talking to Sidekick about your business than having to manipulate your business via the interface.

62
00:05:54,539 --> 00:06:05,880
对，在你加入 Shopify 之前，你和 Molly 一起经营自己的产品设计工作室，那里有非常出色的动效、动画和交互，我一直觉得非常受启发。
Yeah, and previously before joining Shopify, you were building your own product studio with Molly and you have incredible motion and animations and interactions there that I always found to be really inspiring.

63
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:10,040
也许你可以给我们展示一些，并聊聊这些是从哪里来的。
And maybe you can show us some of that and talk through where that came from.

64
00:06:10,200 --> 00:06:10,400
对。
Yeah.

65
00:06:10,600 --> 00:06:16,040
Molly 是我和工作室合伙人 Jay Tal 一起经营的一家布鲁克林设计工作室。
Molly was a studio, a design studio in Brooklyn that I was running together with Jay TEL, my studio partner.

66
00:06:16,880 --> 00:06:23,400
我们经营这个工作室大约两年，然后加入 Shopify，并把工作室整合进 Shopify。
We ran the studio for about two years before joining Shopify and integrating the studio into Shopify.

67
00:06:23,560 --> 00:06:25,240
你们在那段时间合作过一些非常大的客户。
And you had some huge clients that you worked with during that time.

68
00:06:25,760 --> 00:06:31,190
对，我们和 Collins、Apple、Google 合作很多，也有很多更小的客户。
Yeah, we worked, we worked a lot with Collins and Apple and Google and also a lot of smaller clients, too.

69
00:06:31,510 --> 00:06:37,830
说实话，那是一种很广泛的混合，包括网页设计、产品设计，还有很多面向客户的内容策略和信息层级。
It was, it was honestly a broad mix of web design and product design and a lot of content strategy and hierarchy for clients as well.

70
00:06:38,150 --> 00:06:39,670
涉及他们网站的时候。
When it came to their websites.

71
00:06:39,750 --> 00:06:45,910
而且因为我们是小工作室，这让我们能与客户保持非常紧密的关系。
And because we were a small studio, it also allowed us to have extremely close relationships with our clients.

72
00:06:46,110 --> 00:06:54,810
我们会连续很多周每周和客户见面，并把这些 Molly 的项目当成和客户一起高度专注的短期冲刺。
We would meet clients weekly for many weeks and use these projects at Molly as like very, um, high focus stints with these clients.

73
00:06:55,130 --> 00:06:58,530
所以我们一次最多只接两三个客户，以保持高度专注。
Um, so we never took on more than two or three clients at a time to stay really focused.

74
00:06:58,810 --> 00:07:05,090
这里只是我们经营工作室期间所做的一些作品集锦。
Here's just a reel of some of the work we were doing while we were running our studio.

75
00:07:05,130 --> 00:07:05,690
太酷了。
It's so cool.

76
00:07:05,690 --> 00:07:06,890
这里有很多小细节。
There's little details here.

77
00:07:07,410 --> 00:07:09,410
动画非常自然、有机。
Um, the animations are very organic.

78
00:07:09,650 --> 00:07:10,010
对。
Yeah.

79
00:07:10,050 --> 00:07:20,440
而且每当画面切换，它背后的渐变和阴影也会随之变化，以匹配背景颜色，我觉得很多人可能不会注意到这一点。
Um, and even as each screen changes, the gradient and the shadow behind it changes to match the color of the background that I think a lot of people maybe wouldn't pick up on.

80
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:20,840
对。
Yeah.

81
00:07:20,840 --> 00:07:22,880
但这是你会凭直觉感受到的东西。
But it's something that you sort of intuitively feel.

82
00:07:23,240 --> 00:07:23,480
对。
Yeah.

83
00:07:23,600 --> 00:07:24,400
这里正在发生某种变化。
Something is happening here.

84
00:07:24,400 --> 00:07:24,680
没错。
Right.

85
00:07:25,080 --> 00:07:30,000
我们得到这个想法，基本上只是来自电视背后的环境光。
We got this idea basically just from the ambient lighting behind TV.

86
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:43,400
那些安装在墙上的电视，我觉得 Jay Tal——而且这是在 AI 之前——基本上复制了视频，把它模糊处理成背景，这样它就能匹配屏幕上正在发生的事情的节奏。而且它，它——
TVs that are mounted on walls, um, and I think Jay TEL and this was pre-AI, um, basically duplicated the video and had it like blurred out in the background, um, so that it would match the rhythm of what's going on, um, on the screen. And it, and it—

87
00:07:43,400 --> 00:07:51,960
效果非常好，而且我觉得我们——但在这句话之前，它以前只写着 “Molly is a design studio in Brooklyn”，底部只有一个渐变色，有点像是在振动，对吧？
Works really nice and I think what we—but before this sentence, it used to just say "Molly is a design studio in Brooklyn" and there was just the gradient at the bottom that was kind of like vibrating, right?

88
00:07:51,960 --> 00:07:52,800
我能看到它在变化。
I can see it changing.

89
00:07:52,920 --> 00:07:53,720
雷声之类的。
Thunder or something.

90
00:07:53,800 --> 00:07:54,320
对。
Yeah.

91
00:07:54,320 --> 00:07:58,320
至少对我们来说，它总让人觉得人们会倾向于滚动进入其中。
And it, it kind of, at least for us, it always felt like people were inclined to scroll into it.

92
00:07:58,560 --> 00:08:01,720
所以我们甚至不必向人们展示任何作品，只是稍微暗示了一下。
So we didn't even have to show them any of the work, but we kind of hinted at it.

93
00:08:01,880 --> 00:08:02,000
对。
Yeah.

94
00:08:02,040 --> 00:08:08,160
然后你滚动进入作品集锦，剩下就是一些非常好看且简单的轮播组件。
Um, and then you scrolled into the reel and, um, yeah, the rest are just, uh, very nice and simple carousels.

95
00:08:08,280 --> 00:08:13,160
不过，你可以在这里看到，这是我们工作室当时做的一些作品。
But, um, yeah, you can see here, this was some of the work we were doing with our studio.

96
00:08:13,240 --> 00:08:18,840
我觉得你提到的一些模式，我们以前把它称为一个系统——我们称这个系统为 Primal。
And I think some of the patterns you were referring to, um, we used to call the system—we called the system Primal.

97
00:08:19,210 --> 00:08:24,170
它基本上就是一种理解网站内容、消化网站内容的方式。
Um, it's a way to just basically understand content on a website, digest content on a website.

98
00:08:24,370 --> 00:08:35,610
对我们来说，这是一种非常有结构的消化方式。当客户带着大量网站信息找到我们时，我们帮他们把这些信息构建成网站上的一个系统，让用户可以自行探索。
And for us, it was a very structured way of digesting. When clients came to us with a lot of information for their websites, we helped them build that into a system on the website that allowed users to explore.

99
00:08:36,010 --> 00:08:44,530
对我们来说，我们构建网站的方式，以及你在网站中流动的感觉，更像是你在探索网站，而不是网站强求你的注意力。
To us, it always felt like the way we would build websites and you would flow through the website felt more like you were exploring the website than the website was demanding your attention.

100
00:08:45,010 --> 00:08:51,930
所有内容都只隔着一层，所有内容都渐进式地展开，所以你永远不会用太多内容让用户不堪重负。
Everything is just one layer away and everything is progressively disclosed so you never overwhelm the user with too much content.

101
00:08:51,930 --> 00:08:54,610
总的来说，你们是如何做到这一点的？
In general, what are some of the ways that you accomplish that?

102
00:08:54,730 --> 00:08:56,330
我的意思是，我可以直接看一些作品。
I mean, I can just go through some of the work.

103
00:08:56,410 --> 00:08:59,730
这是我们和 Collins 代理机构一起做的一个项目。
This was a project we did with the agency Collins.

104
00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:05,000
我觉得我们和他们在这个网站上合作了一年多。
And we worked with them for, I think, over a year, um, on this website.

105
00:09:05,120 --> 00:09:15,760
正如你看到的，当你在首页滚动时，首页的每个区块就像一个章节，随着你与网站交互，它可以引导你进入更深入的探索。
And as you can see, even as you're scrolling through the homepage here, each section of the homepage is a chapter that, as you interact with the website, can lead you into further exploration.

106
00:09:15,880 --> 00:09:17,920
这是我最喜欢的区块之一。
So this is one of my favorite sections here.

107
00:09:17,960 --> 00:09:19,000
这是书架。
This is the bookshelf.

108
00:09:19,080 --> 00:09:27,630
