00:18:24,340 --> 00:18:37,100
具体到这个案例，后台有一个供开发使用的 playground（试验环境）。我们在那里测试，里面的代码不是生产代码，也不能合入 Shopify 的主 GitHub 仓库，也就是主代码库。
In this specific case, admin has a playground that we build inside of. In that playground we test—that's not production code and that wouldn't be allowed back into the main GitHub repo of Shopify, into like our main repository.

215
00:18:37,500 --> 00:18:47,780
不过话虽如此，AI 确实已经发展到这样一个阶段：作为设计师，我们非常有能力和条件把任何想法可视化，把自己的想法表达清楚。
Having said that, though, AI has definitely gotten to the point now where as designers, we are highly empowered and capable to visualize any idea that we want to get our idea across.

216
00:18:48,100 --> 00:18:50,020
在以前的世界里，这根本不可能。
In a previous world, that just wasn't possible.

217
00:18:50,100 --> 00:18:58,180
我们之前看过 Collins 网站。我们当时在做的很多 Figma 工作，看起来仍然是这样的。
We looked at the Collins website earlier. A lot of that work in Figma when we worked on it still looks like this.

218
00:18:58,580 --> 00:19:04,540
你可以放大这里任何部分，它们都是非常详细的 Figma 界面，我们就是在这些界面里做设计。
You can zoom into any part here and they're like highly detailed Figma screens that we're designing through.

219
00:19:04,540 --> 00:19:05,900
这是一个构思过程。
And this is an ideation process.

220
00:19:06,260 --> 00:19:12,280
我记得这是我、Michael Speicher 和 Jay Tal 一起合作这个项目的成果。
I think this is a mixture of me, Michael Speicher, and Jay Tal working on this project together.

221
00:19:12,600 --> 00:19:18,200
你能看到整个演变过程，比如我们如何过各种想法、尝试新东西。
And you can just see the evolution of, like, as we're going through ideas and like trying out new things.

222
00:19:18,400 --> 00:19:21,520
这些在代码里测试太重、成本太高。
These are too heavy and too expensive to test in code.

223
00:19:21,800 --> 00:19:27,720
我们现在不想在代码里测试这个，因为那需要工程师来看并帮我们实现。
We don't want to test this in code right now because it would require an engineer to take a look at it and build it for us.

224
00:19:27,800 --> 00:19:29,000
所以我们全都在 Figma 里测试。
So we test it all in Figma.

225
00:19:29,080 --> 00:19:31,440
Figma 就是我们的试验场，也是我们的整个世界。
Figma is the playground and the world.

226
00:19:31,600 --> 00:19:35,680
我们当时在所有东西上都是先做桌面端，然后再把它转换到移动端。
We were building everything in—we were building it on desktop and then we were translating it to mobile.

227
00:19:35,760 --> 00:19:43,840
而且所有这些东西都必须先在 Figma 里做很长时间的压力测试，之后我们才会去频繁打扰工程团队。
And all of this had to be stress tested out in Figma first for a very long time before we would go and bug the engineering team a lot of the time.

228
00:19:44,040 --> 00:19:50,720
我目前和 AI 协作、或者仍然使用 Figma 的方式是，我可以只凭一个非常粗略的草图就推进得很远。
The way I'm talking to AI or still using Figma at the moment is I can get really far with a very broad sketch.

229
00:19:51,120 --> 00:20:00,530
举个例子，这是我周末一直在做的一个业余项目，我可以稍微展开讲讲，但我想为这个项目做的东西之一是……
Here's an example of a side project I've been working on over the weekend, which I can go into detail a bit, but one of the things I wanted to build out for this project was a...

230
00:20:00,730 --> 00:20:04,730
一个 2 乘 3 的概览，用来展示这些草地研究和拍的视频。
A 2 by 3 overview of these grass studies and these videos I took.

231
00:20:05,130 --> 00:20:10,130
这里的交互是，你可以打开任意一个视频，然后向下拖动来关闭它。
And the interaction here is you can open up any one of these videos and you can drag down to close them.

232
00:20:10,530 --> 00:20:13,690
在模拟器里也是同样的效果。
And it works the same in the simulator.

233
00:20:14,190 --> 00:20:21,590
我向 AI 解释、描述了这个需求，并且我在 Codex 里工作，Codex 帮我把它做了出来。
I explained this, I described this to AI, and I was working in Codex and Codex built this out for me.

234
00:20:21,870 --> 00:20:25,190
但那个交互不是可中断的。
But the interaction wasn't interruptible.

235
00:20:25,270 --> 00:20:34,790
所以每次我点击一个元素并把它拉下来，都必须等它完全回到原位并停好，之后才能点开另一张图片。
So every time I clicked on an asset and pulled it down, I had to wait until the asset was all the way back in its position and set down before I was able to tap on another image.

236
00:20:34,870 --> 00:20:37,310
这不是你希望使用这种交互模式的方式。
And that's just not how you want to use this pattern.

237
00:20:37,470 --> 00:20:42,910
它必须响应非常快，否则你很快就会对界面感到烦躁。
This has to be very responsive, very quickly, otherwise you'll get very annoyed with the interface very fast.

238
00:20:43,390 --> 00:20:52,830
所以我就进到 Figma 里，画了一些非常基础的简单方框：比如点击方框时，红色会弹出来，你可以把它滑走。
So I just went into Figma, kind of drew out very rudimentary simple boxes like, when you click on the box, red comes up, you can swipe it away.

239
00:20:52,910 --> 00:20:57,830
如果在滑走的时候点击蓝色，蓝色就会弹出来，你可以再把它点掉，然后对绿色也做同样操作。
If, as you're swiping away, you click on blue, blue now comes up and you can click that away and you do it with green.

240
00:20:57,910 --> 00:21:10,840
我觉得这是一个很好的例子，说明至少就我个人而言，我现在更多把 Figma 当作一个草稿本，用来梳理想法，然后再把这些想法交给 Codex，然后我……
And I think that's just like a good example of how, at least myself personally, I'm using Figma more—it's more a scratch pad to like map out ideas and I give those ideas to Codex and I...

241
00:21:10,840 --> 00:21:17,080
我用草稿本，是因为这样更方便自己拆解想法，也方便 Codex 理解和拆解。
And I use the Scratch Pad, a, because it's easier for me to, to decompile something, but also it's actually easy for Codex to decompile it.

242
00:21:17,080 --> 00:21:19,200
如果它从视觉上看到它，就会解读那个图像。
If it sees it visually, it interprets that image.

243
00:21:19,640 --> 00:21:21,560
我觉得这和我解读它的方式类似。
I think similar to how I interpreted it.

244
00:21:22,020 --> 00:21:25,540
我，至少它理解我在做什么，以及我是怎么思考这个动效的。
I, I, at least it understood what I was doing and how I was thinking about the motion.

245
00:21:25,780 --> 00:21:29,340
我没法通过文字或语音表达清楚，但我能把它画出来。
I couldn't articulate it via, via text or voice, but I could draw it out.

246
00:21:29,580 --> 00:21:32,060
我认为这里面有两点值得强调。
There's two parts of this that I think are important to call out.

247
00:21:32,380 --> 00:21:38,860
我觉得第一，Codex 以及当前所有 AI 软件和模型都极其擅长创造任何东西。
I think No. 1, Codex and all the current AI software is, is, and the models are extremely capable at creating anything.

248
00:21:39,220 --> 00:21:41,460
你可以用这些工具做出任何你想要的东西。
You can use these tools to make anything that you want.

249
00:21:41,740 --> 00:21:42,940
但你必须知道自己在做什么。
But you have to know what you're doing.

250
00:21:43,260 --> 00:21:56,820
而且你必须能把一个概念，或像 shader（着色器）这样更复杂的东西，拆成各个小部分；理解相关指令后，再把这些部分组合起来。对，给我们展示一下你是怎么做的。
And you have to be able to take a, a concept or, or something more complicated like the shader and break it down into all its little components, you have to be able to understand those commands and then you can put those components back together. Yeah. Just show us how you did that.

251
00:21:56,820 --> 00:22:02,420
是这样的，为了明确，你有一堆图像和视频，以及……的视频。
Here because, so to be clear, you had a bunch of images and videos and videos of.

252
00:22:02,780 --> 00:22:04,140
实际草地的视频。
Of actual grass.

253
00:22:04,380 --> 00:22:04,660
对。
Yeah.

254
00:22:04,820 --> 00:22:06,500
然后你想创建一个 shader。
And then you wanted to create a shader.

255
00:22:06,700 --> 00:22:07,100
是的。
Yes.

256
00:22:07,100 --> 00:22:10,660
它看起来要跟那个一模一样，但你可以像 shader 一样控制和操作。
That looked identical to that, but you could control and function like a shader.

257
00:22:10,820 --> 00:22:16,260
那谈谈你是怎么处理不同元素，以及经历了哪些过程，才让最终效果看起来很逼真。
And so talk about how, like, the different elements and what you had to go through to actually get that to look lifelike.

258
00:22:16,260 --> 00:22:17,780
我这里有几张截图。
I have a few screenshots here.

259
00:22:17,780 --> 00:22:20,380
这又是一个我如何使用 Figma 的好例子。
Again, this is a good example of how I use, of how I use Figma.

260
00:22:20,700 --> 00:22:29,540
我第一次把视频输入 Codex，然后向它解释我的项目和我想要经历的过程：拿这个视频。
So I fed the video into, into, into Codex for the first time and just explained it, my, the, the, the project and the process that I was, I wanted to go through, take the video.

261
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:30,960
把它变成一个 shader。
Turn this into a shader.

262
00:22:31,480 --> 00:22:33,240
我想让 shader 在网页中运行。
I want the shader running, running in the web.

263
00:22:33,720 --> 00:22:36,680
我希望你尽可能地细致。
And I want you to be as, as, as meticulous as you can be.

264
00:22:36,680 --> 00:22:37,600
非常像素级精确。
Very pixel perfect.

265
00:22:38,400 --> 00:22:43,880
抓取所有颜色，确保使用相同的 hex 色值，理解图像的每一个部分，然后尝试重新创建它。
Grab all the colors, make sure that you're using the same hex codes, understand each part of the image and then try to recreate that.

266
00:22:43,920 --> 00:22:45,200
然后它就去做了，说“没问题”。
And it went off and said, no problem.

267
00:22:45,240 --> 00:22:45,720
交给我。
I got you.

268
00:22:45,720 --> 00:22:50,760
然后它得出了这个，显然跟我们最终达到、最终的效果完全不像。
And it came out with this, which obviously doesn't look at all where we landed, where we ended up now.

269
00:22:51,440 --> 00:22:57,520
但从这里开始，我的流程是截图这个结果和我最初的视频。
But then the process from here was I would screenshot this and my original, my original video.

270
00:22:58,040 --> 00:23:01,320
我会打开 ChatGPT，把它交给那里的另一个 Agent。
I would open up ChatGPT and I gave it to another agent there.

271
00:23:01,560 --> 00:23:03,440
我向那个 Agent 准确解释我在做什么。
I explained that agent exactly what I was doing.

272
00:23:03,440 --> 00:23:06,440
然后我说，为什么另一个 Agent 理解不了我想达到的效果？
And I said, why is the other agent not understanding what I'm trying to get to?

273
00:23:06,440 --> 00:23:18,760
然后那个 ChatGPT 帮助我理解了自己的流程，比如：你必须告诉它光线来自哪里，你使用了哪些阴影，也许可以把这个图像拆分成六个部分，让它分别处理每一部分。
And that ChatGPT then helped me kind of like understand my own process like, well, you have to explain to it where the light is coming from and what, what shadows you're using and maybe break the image actually down into like six parts and have it do each one of those individually.

274
00:23:18,920 --> 00:23:19,680
然后重新组合在一起。
And put that back together.

275
00:23:20,020 --> 00:23:29,820
当我思考这些不同的事情时，我在尝试想清楚如何对 AI 变得非常明确，向它准确解释它到底在看什么。
And as I was kind of thinking about all of these different things, like I was trying to come up with how could I become very clear with the, with, with AI and explain to it exactly what it was actually looking at.

276
00:23:30,260 --> 00:23:38,460
所以我用的一个技巧是，进入 ChatGPT，让它基本上渲染一张底层结构的地形图。
So one of the tricks I used was I went into ChatGPT and I had it render basically a topographical map of the underlying structure AI.

277
00:23:39,020 --> 00:23:45,630
随着我不断推进，它逐渐更接近了，你可以看到它在这里给草叶填充了更多像素。
As I was going through this, it was kind of getting closer and you could see that, you know, it was painting more pixels into, into the grass blades here.

278
00:23:45,630 --> 00:23:49,390
这些开始看起来有点像草丛，感觉更真实了。
And these started to look like bushels a little bit and which which felt more realistic.

279
00:23:49,470 --> 00:23:58,630
然后在这里，我想，好，我们有点进展了，现在这个看起来不再很糟糕；但我发现它不明白底下不是一个平面层。
And then over here, we, I was like, okay, we're getting somewhere here now this doesn't look, this doesn't look terrible anymore but I kind of figured out it doesn't understand that there's, it's not a flat layer underneath.

280
00:23:58,710 --> 00:24:00,030
这些是小山丘，对吧？
These are little hills, right?

281
00:24:00,200 --> 00:24:03,880
基本上，我把这个反馈给 AI，它就明白了。
Basically, I fed this back into into AI and it, and it understood.

282
00:24:03,960 --> 00:24:04,400
哦，好的。
Oh, okay.

283
00:24:04,440 --> 00:24:08,600
所以地图里有一个底层结构，具有不同的深度和高度。
So there's, there's a, an underlying structure in a map that has different depths and heights.

284
00:24:08,920 --> 00:24:17,800
如果我能在这上面，在那个基础设施或整体结构之上重新渲染草地，对我来说创建场景会容易得多。
And if I can rerender the grass on top of those and on top of that infrastructure or that structure in general, it'll be much easier for, for me to, to create the scene.

285
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:19,200
这样效果真的很好。
And that kind of worked really well.

286
00:24:19,240 --> 00:24:29,520
在这个原型里，你其实可以——有一个二元开关来打开或关闭草地，而且你还可以在这里对地形进行不少调整。
And, um, you know, in this, in this prototype, you can actually binary, there's a binary toggle to turn the grass on or off and you can actually play with the terrain quite a bit here as well.

287
00:24:29,640 --> 00:24:29,920
对。
Right.

288
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:38,920
前几天吃午饭时，Josh 帮我打开了思路。我们把 Dialkit（参数调节工具）接入这个演示，他看了之后说：效果不错。
This was all unlocked via Josh to me at a lunch the other day where he kind of like, we installed Dialkit into this demo and because he looked at this, like, this looks pretty good.

289
00:24:38,960 --> 00:24:40,680
但你应该看看草叶的长度。
But you should look at the length of the grass blades.

290
00:24:40,760 --> 00:24:41,690
我当时的反应是：哇。
I was like, whoa.

291
00:24:41,770 --> 00:24:42,490
好，有道理。
Okay, that's true.

292
00:24:42,570 --> 00:24:47,210
于是我问 Codex，嘿，我们有多少种草叶变化？
So I asked, I asked Codex, like, hey, how many, how many grass blade variations do we have?

293
00:24:47,450 --> 00:24:53,450
它说大约有217,000片草叶，但它们的差异只有0.6、0.7、0.8这样。
And it said we have about 217,000 different grass blades, but they only differ by, like, point six, point seven, point eight.

294
00:24:53,810 --> 00:24:56,570
所以我们想，好吧，那我们就为它做一个开关，这样我们自己就能看到。
So we were like, okay, let's just build a toggle for it so that we can see it ourselves.

295
00:24:56,850 --> 00:25:02,650
我不想再在聊天界面里来回描述了，直接把底层结构展示出来。
I don't want to have to go back and forth with the interface and, and chat about this. Expose the underlying structure.

296
00:25:03,580 --> 00:25:06,780
把底层结构展示出来，也让我看清了 AI 在哪里出了错。
Exposing the underlying structure would also show me where AI had made mistakes.

297
00:25:07,100 --> 00:25:11,020
它最初实现的地形图版本完全错误、扭曲了。
The original version of the terrain map that it had implemented was completely wrong and, and, and skewed.

298
00:25:11,500 --> 00:25:17,460
所以无论如何我都无法达到想要的结果，因为 AI 在 5 条提示之前就误解了某些东西，而我当时并不知道。
So I couldn't get to my outcome anyway because AI had misinterpreted something 5 prompts ago that I didn't know about.

299
00:25:17,540 --> 00:25:19,900
总之，Dialkit 能帮我把这些拆开看清楚。
Anyway, Dialkit could kind of unpack that for me.

300
00:25:20,260 --> 00:25:30,040
但总的来说，你只需调整其中几个数值，它们就能帮助把一些……它们能帮助把我真正想看的东西可视化出来。
But then in general, you can just mess with a few of these values and they kind of help like bring, bring some of this, uh, like they, they, they help visualize what, what, what I really wanted to see.

301
00:25:30,160 --> 00:25:34,440
如果这里的长度变长，你就可以引入更多风。
And if, you know, the length here is longer, then you can introduce more wind.

302
00:25:34,480 --> 00:25:40,520
我想测试一下，看看我能把风的运动调得多夸张。
This was something I wanted to test and see how crazy I could get with the wind movement.

303
00:25:40,560 --> 00:25:41,880
可以看到它变得有点狂野。
You can see it gets a little wild.

304
00:25:42,200 --> 00:25:57,520
但我觉得这只是一个小例子，一个有点好玩的项目，就是想试试用这些工具能走多远。这些事我觉得在 AI 之前我做不到。我只是——我可能——
But yeah, I think this is just a small example of a little bit of a fun project, just to try and see how far you can get with the tools. And these are things that, um, I think before AI, I couldn't do. I just—I might have—

305
00:25:57,520 --> 00:26:01,480
有过这些想法，但没被允许，或者说没有能力把它们实现出来。
Had these ideas, but I wasn't allowed or able to execute on them.

306
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:05,280
我觉得这就是总体上设计角色正在发生变化的地方。
And I think that's just in general what's changing with a lot of the design role.

307
00:26:05,640 --> 00:26:06,240
就在当下。
Um, right now.

308
00:26:06,240 --> 00:26:06,960
对。
Yeah.

309
00:26:06,960 --> 00:26:11,820
总之，你们设计师正变得更全栈，工程师和 PM 也一样。
Anyway, like, it's—you designers are becoming more full stack, as are engineers and PMs.

310
00:26:11,940 --> 00:26:13,700
它会向各个方向扩展。
It goes in every which way.

311
00:26:13,700 --> 00:26:15,300
是啊，现在已经没有限制了。
Yeah, there's no limitations now.

312
00:26:15,300 --> 00:26:15,500
对吧？
Right?

313
00:26:15,500 --> 00:26:27,220
实际上，这也是我们最近经常看到的一种常见模式：先做原型，然后做一些小部件，让你可以控制其中的不同变量，以便——
And actually, this is one of the common patterns that we've been seeing a lot: creating prototypes and then creating little widgets that allow you to control different variables in them to—

314
00:26:27,380 --> 00:26:29,340
能够非常容易地测试并把它调好。
To be able to test it really easily and get it right.

315
00:26:29,500 --> 00:26:29,740
对。
Yeah.

316
00:26:29,740 --> 00:26:31,940
以前，这不值得任何人花时间。
And previously, it wouldn't have been worth anybody's time.

317
00:26:32,140 --> 00:26:32,300
对。
Yeah.

318
00:26:32,300 --> 00:26:32,740
去做这件事。
To do that.

319
00:26:32,980 --> 00:26:37,620
你可能只是在代码里调调变量之类的，直到差不多，然后就说行了。
You would just, you know, maybe play with variables in code or something until you got it, and then you'd call it good.

320
00:26:38,080 --> 00:26:41,320
而且那会花长得多的时间，或者你根本不会花时间去做。
Um, and that would take way longer or you wouldn't even spend the time doing it.

321
00:26:41,640 --> 00:26:48,480
现在很容易创建新的小软件工具来控制这些变量、测试并把它调到完美。
Now it's so easy to create new little software tools to be able to control these variables and test things and dial it in perfectly.

322
00:26:48,800 --> 00:26:51,200
对，我猜这就是 Dialkit 这个名字的由来。
